<div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto py-12 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
  <div class="mb-8">
    <a href="/docs/sdbql" class="inline-flex items-center text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 font-medium transition-colors group">
      <svg class="w-4 h-4 mr-2 transform group-hover:-translate-x-1 transition-transform" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"></path>
      </svg>
      Back to SDBQL Reference
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-12">
    <main class="lg:col-span-12 space-y-12">
      <!-- Header -->
      <div class="relative">
        <div class="absolute -inset-1 bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-500 to-amber-600 rounded-lg blur opacity-25"></div>
        <div class="relative">
          <h1 class="text-5xl font-extrabold text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-400 via-amber-400 to-yellow-400 mb-4 tracking-tight">
            Aggregations
          </h1>
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
            COLLECT for grouping, AGGREGATE for computing SUM, AVG, COUNT, MIN, MAX and statistical functions, and ROLLUP, CUBE and GROUPING() for subtotals.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Group & Aggregate -->
      <section id="group-aggregate" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-900/40 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/10 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-2xl">
          <div class="p-8 border-b border-white/5 bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-500/10 to-amber-500/10">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white flex items-center">
              <svg class="w-6 h-6 mr-3 text-orange-400" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 11H5m14 0a2 2 0 012 2v6a2 2 0 01-2 2H5a2 2 0 01-2-2v-6a2 2 0 012-2m14 0V9a2 2 0 00-2-2M5 11V9a2 2 0 012-2m0 0V5a2 2 0 012-2h6a2 2 0 012 2v2M7 7h10"></path>
              </svg>
              Group & Aggregate
            </h2>
          </div>
          <div class="p-8 space-y-8">
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
              Use the <code>COLLECT</code> clause to group documents and perform aggregations like counting, summing, or averaging.
            </p>

            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
              <!-- Basic Grouping -->
              <div class="bg-gray-800/30 rounded-xl border border-white/5 overflow-hidden">
                <div class="p-5 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
                  <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-orange-300 flex items-center">Basic Grouping</h3>
                </div>
                <div class="p-5 space-y-4">
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">Group results by one or more fields to find distinct values.</p>
                  <pre><code class="language-sql text-sm">FOR doc IN users
  COLLECT city = doc.city
  RETURN { city }</code></pre>
                </div>
              </div>

              <!-- With COUNT -->
              <div class="bg-gray-800/30 rounded-xl border border-white/5 overflow-hidden">
                <div class="p-5 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
                  <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-orange-300 flex items-center">Counting Groups</h3>
                </div>
                <div class="p-5 space-y-4">
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">Count the number of items in each group using <code>WITH COUNT INTO</code>.</p>
                  <pre><code class="language-sql text-sm">FOR doc IN users
  COLLECT city = doc.city
  WITH COUNT INTO count
  RETURN { city, count }</code></pre>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- With Aggregation -->
            <div class="bg-gray-800/30 rounded-xl border border-white/5 overflow-hidden">
              <div class="p-5 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
                <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-orange-300 flex items-center">Aggregation Functions</h3>
              </div>
              <div class="p-5 space-y-4">
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">Calculate aggregates using functions like <code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>.</p>
                <pre><code class="language-sql text-sm">FOR s IN sales
  COLLECT product = s.product
  AGGREGATE
    total = SUM(s.amount),
    average = AVG(s.amount),
    min_amt = MIN(s.amount),
    max_amt = MAX(s.amount)
  RETURN { product, total, average, min_amt, max_amt }</code></pre>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Collecting Lists -->
            <div class="bg-gray-800/30 rounded-xl border border-white/5 overflow-hidden">
              <div class="p-5 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
                <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-orange-300 flex items-center">Collecting Lists</h3>
              </div>
              <div class="p-5 space-y-4">
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">Use <code>COLLECT_LIST</code> to aggregate values from the group into an array.</p>
                <pre><code class="language-sql text-sm">FOR u IN users
  COLLECT city = u.city
  AGGREGATE userNames = COLLECT_LIST(u.name)
  RETURN {
    city,
    userNames
  }</code></pre>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- KEEP restriction -->
            <div class="bg-gray-800/30 rounded-xl border border-white/5 overflow-hidden">
              <div class="p-5 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
                <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-orange-300 flex items-center">Restricting Group Items with KEEP</h3>
              </div>
              <div class="p-5 space-y-4">
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
                  With <code>INTO groups</code>, each group array normally stores every variable in
                  scope. Add <code class="text-orange-300">KEEP v1, v2</code> to store only those
                  variables — smaller results, no leaked document bodies.
                </p>
                <pre><code class="language-sql text-sm">FOR u IN users
  LET n = u.name
  COLLECT city = u.city INTO g KEEP n
  RETURN { city, names: g[*].n }</code></pre>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
                  Without <code>KEEP</code>, each item of <code>g</code> would also carry <code>u</code>.
                  Every name listed must be a variable in scope at the <code>COLLECT</code> — an
                  unknown name is an error, not an empty group item.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- AGGREGATE reference -->
            <div class="bg-gray-800/30 rounded-xl border border-white/5 overflow-hidden">
              <div class="p-5 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
                <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-orange-300 flex items-center">Functions Available in AGGREGATE</h3>
              </div>
              <div class="p-5 space-y-4 overflow-x-auto">
                <table class="w-full text-sm">
                  <tbody>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">COUNT()</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Number of rows in the group.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">COUNT(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Rows where <code>expr</code> is not null.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">LENGTH(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Number of rows (AQL: same as <code>COUNT()</code>).</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">SUM(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Sum of the numeric values; other values are skipped. The values are added exactly and the total is rounded once, so the result is the correctly rounded sum whatever order the documents are read in (ten <code>0.1</code> make <code>1.0</code>).</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">AVG(expr) / AVERAGE(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Mean of the numeric values (their exact sum, rounded once, divided by their count); <code>null</code> when there are none.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">MIN(expr) / MINIMUM(expr)<br>MAX(expr) / MAXIMUM(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Smallest / largest non-null value in AQL type order; the stored value is returned unchanged.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">COUNT_DISTINCT(expr) / COUNT_UNIQUE(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Number of distinct values (<code>1</code> and <code>1.0</code> are the same value).</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">UNIQUE(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">The distinct values, as an array, in first-seen order.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">SORTED_UNIQUE(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">The distinct values, sorted.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">COLLECT_LIST(expr) / PUSH(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Every value, as an array, in row order.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">VARIANCE(expr) / VARIANCE_POPULATION(expr)<br>VARIANCE_SAMPLE(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Population / sample variance of the numeric values, from their exact sum and the exact sum of their squares: the same number whatever order the documents are read in.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">STDDEV(expr) / STDDEV_POPULATION(expr)<br>STDDEV_SAMPLE(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Population / sample standard deviation.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">MEDIAN(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Median of the numeric values.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">PERCENTILE(expr, p) / QUANTILE(expr, p)<br>PERCENTILE(expr, p, "interpolation")</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">The <code>p</code>-th percentile (0 to 100) of the numeric values: nearest rank, or interpolated between the closest ranks. <code>p</code> is a number or a bind variable, not an expression of the row; a rank outside 0 to 100 gives <code>null</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">An estimate of the number of distinct values (null is one of them), from a HyperLogLog sketch: at most 16&nbsp;KB per group however many values. A float.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">BIT_AND(expr)<br>BIT_OR(expr)<br>BIT_XOR(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Bitwise fold of the integer values; nulls are skipped, any other non-integer makes the result <code>null</code>.</td></tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
                  These mean the same thing whatever the collection's storage layout.
                  <code>PERCENTILE</code> and <code>APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT</code> answer what the array functions of
                  those names answer over the group's values (<code>COLLECT … INTO g = u.amount</code>, then
                  <code>PERCENTILE(g, 95)</code>), without holding the values as rows.
                </p>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
                  Groups are returned in ascending order of their group values. A <code>ROLLUP</code> or
                  <code>CUBE</code> sorts the detail groups first and the grand total last: by how many
                  variables were rolled up, then by the same key order, and a null sorts below every value.
                  A <code>COLLECT</code> with no group variable always returns exactly one row, even over no
                  input, and <code>ROLLUP</code> or <code>CUBE</code> with no group variable is that same one
                  row: <code>FOR u IN [] COLLECT WITH COUNT INTO n RETURN n</code> is <code>[0]</code>.
                  With group variables, an empty input yields no rows at all, grand total included.
                </p>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
                  Memory: a <code>COLLECT</code> holds one row per group, plus what <code>INTO</code> and
                  <code>COLLECT_LIST</code>, <code>UNIQUE</code>, <code>SORTED_UNIQUE</code>,
                  <code>COUNT_DISTINCT</code>, <code>MEDIAN</code> and <code>PERCENTILE</code> keep (the numbers
                  of the last two count for an eighth of a row each against
                  <code>SOLIDB_MAX_INTERMEDIATE_ROWS</code>). When it reads a collection scan
                  directly &mdash; <code>FOR x IN c</code>, then only <code>FILTER</code> and <code>LET</code>
                  clauses, then the <code>COLLECT</code> &mdash; each document is grouped as it is read and
                  dropped, and only the fields of <code>x</code> the clauses use are decoded. The collection's
                  size then neither grows memory nor counts against <code>SOLIDB_MAX_INTERMEDIATE_ROWS</code>;
                  only the rows the <code>COLLECT</code> keeps do. EXPLAIN reports it as
                  <code>COLLECT folded into the scan of 'c'</code> in <code>warnings</code>. A loop whose
                  <code>FILTER</code> an index serves reads the matching documents first, and a columnar,
                  sharded or search-view source, or a <code>SYSTEM_TIME</code> / valid-time loop, is read whole
                  before grouping.
                </p>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
                  Speed: when the group keys and aggregate arguments are fields of <code>x</code>
                  (<code>x.status</code>, <code>x.address.city</code>) and each <code>FILTER</code> compares a
                  field with a constant or bind variable (<code>==</code>, <code>!=</code>, <code>&lt;</code>,
                  <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>IN</code>, <code>NOT IN</code>,
                  joined by <code>AND</code>, <code>OR</code>, <code>NOT</code>), those fields are read straight
                  from the stored bytes: nothing is decoded into a document, and a document that joins an existing
                  group allocates nothing. EXPLAIN then says <code>reading only amount, status from the stored
                  bytes</code>. Anything else (a function call, a <code>LET</code>, <code>INTO</code>, a system field
                  such as <code>_key</code>) decodes the fields it needs, with the same result.
                </p>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
                  Threads: on an engine that can read several key ranges at one snapshot (both do), such a scan over
                  a large collection is split into key ranges read on several threads and the partial groups are
                  merged &mdash; when every aggregate merges exactly: <code>COUNT</code>, <code>SUM</code>,
                  <code>AVG</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>, <code>COUNT_DISTINCT</code>, the variance
                  family, <code>MEDIAN</code>, <code>PERCENTILE</code>, <code>APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT</code> and the
                  <code>BIT_*</code> functions (lists and <code>UNIQUE</code> stay on one thread). The answer is the one-thread answer, to the bit: sums are exact, and of equal
                  extremes or equal keys (<code>1</code> and <code>1.0</code>) the first document's is kept. EXPLAIN
                  adds <code>4 key ranges read in parallel</code>. <code>SOLIDB_SCAN_THREADS</code> sets how many
                  threads a scan may use, its own included (by default the CPUs the server may use); the extra
                  threads of all scans running at once never exceed that number less one, so concurrent
                  aggregations share the CPUs instead of multiplying the load. On solistore the key ranges
                  are the engine's own split points, taken from its table indexes and memtables.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Syntax Reference -->
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-lg border border-white/5 p-4 mt-8">
              <code class="text-sm text-gray-300 font-mono block whitespace-pre overflow-x-auto">COLLECT <span class="text-orange-400">variable</span> = <span class="text-purple-400">expression</span> [, ...]
[ROLLUP | CUBE]
[INTO <span class="text-orange-400">groupVariable</span> [KEEP <span class="text-orange-400">var1</span>, <span class="text-orange-400">var2</span>, ...]]
[WITH COUNT INTO <span class="text-orange-400">countVariable</span>]
[AGGREGATE <span class="text-orange-400">variable</span> = <span class="text-blue-400">FUNC</span>(<span class="text-purple-400">expression</span>) [, ...]]</code>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Aggregation Functions -->
      <section id="aggregation-functions" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-900/40 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/10 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-2xl">
          <div class="p-8 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white flex items-center">Aggregation Functions</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="p-8 space-y-8">
            <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">Basic Aggregations</h4>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
              <div id="fn-SUM" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                <a href="#fn-SUM" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-orange-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">SUM(arr)</code>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Sum of all values in the array.</p>
                <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN SUM([1, 2, 3, 4]) --10</code></pre>
              </div>
              <div id="fn-AVG" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                <a href="#fn-AVG" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-orange-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">AVG(arr)</code>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Average of all values.</p>
                <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN AVG([1, 2, 3, 4]) --2.5</code></pre>
              </div>
              <div id="fn-MIN" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                <a href="#fn-MIN" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-orange-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">MIN(arr)</code>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Smallest value in the array.</p>
                <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN MIN([5, 1, 9]) --1</code></pre>
              </div>
              <div id="fn-MAX" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                <a href="#fn-MAX" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-orange-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">MAX(arr)</code>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Largest value in the array.</p>
                <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN MAX([5, 1, 9]) --9</code></pre>
              </div>
              <div id="fn-COUNT" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                <a href="#fn-COUNT" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-orange-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">COUNT(arr)</code>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Number of elements in the array.</p>
                <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN COUNT([1, 2, 3]) --3</code></pre>
              </div>
              <div id="fn-COUNT_DISTINCT" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                <a href="#fn-COUNT_DISTINCT" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-orange-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">COUNT_DISTINCT(arr)</code>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Number of unique elements.</p>
                <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN COUNT_DISTINCT([1, 2, 2, 3]) --3</code></pre>
              </div>
              <div id="fn-MEDIAN" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                <a href="#fn-MEDIAN" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-orange-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">MEDIAN(arr)</code>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Median value of the array.</p>
                <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN MEDIAN([1, 5, 10]) --5</code></pre>
              </div>
              <div id="fn-PERCENTILE" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                <a href="#fn-PERCENTILE" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-orange-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">PERCENTILE(arr, p [, method])</code>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Returns the p-th percentile (<code class="text-xs">p</code> in 0-100). <code class="text-xs">method</code> is <code class="text-xs">"rank"</code> (default) or <code class="text-xs">"interpolation"</code>.</p>
                <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN PERCENTILE([1..100], 95) --95
RETURN PERCENTILE([1, 2, 3, 4], 50, "interpolation") --2.5</code></pre>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">Statistical Functions</h4>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
                <div id="fn-VARIANCE" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                  <a href="#fn-VARIANCE" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-blue-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                  <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">VARIANCE(arr) / VARIANCE_POPULATION(arr)</code>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Population variance: 0 for one value, <code>null</code> for none.</p>
                  <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN VARIANCE([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) --2</code></pre>
                </div>
              <div id="fn-VARIANCE_SAMPLE" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                <a href="#fn-VARIANCE_SAMPLE" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-orange-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">VARIANCE_SAMPLE(arr)</code>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Sample variance.</p>
                <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN VARIANCE_SAMPLE([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) --2.5</code></pre>
              </div>
                <div id="fn-STDDEV" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                  <a href="#fn-STDDEV" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-blue-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                  <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">STDDEV(arr)</code>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3"><strong>Population</strong> standard deviation, as in AQL (same as <code>STDDEV_POPULATION</code>).</p>
                  <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN STDDEV([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) --1.414...</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div id="fn-STDDEV_POPULATION" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                  <a href="#fn-STDDEV_POPULATION" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-blue-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                  <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">STDDEV_POPULATION(arr)</code>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Population standard deviation.</p>
                  <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN STDDEV_POPULATION([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) --1.414...</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div id="fn-STDDEV_SAMPLE" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                  <a href="#fn-STDDEV_SAMPLE" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-blue-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                  <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">STDDEV_SAMPLE(arr)</code>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Sample standard deviation; <code>null</code> for fewer than two values.</p>
                  <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN STDDEV_SAMPLE([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) --1.581...</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-800/30 rounded-xl p-5 border border-white/5">
              <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-300 mb-3">Aggregation Example</h4>
              <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-3 rounded border border-white/5">FOR u IN users
  LET amounts = (FOR o IN orders FILTER o.user == u._key RETURN o.amount)
  RETURN {
    user: u.name,
    total: SUM(amounts),
    avg: ROUND(AVG(amounts), 2),
    count: COUNT(amounts),
    median: MEDIAN(amounts),
    stddev: ROUND(STDDEV(amounts), 2)
  }</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Approximate Aggregates -->
      <section id="approximate" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-900/40 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/10 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-2xl">
          <div class="p-8 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white flex items-center">Approximate Aggregates</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="p-8">
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                <div id="fn-APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                  <a href="#fn-APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-blue-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                  <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT(arr, options?)</code>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">HyperLogLog (p = 14) estimate of the number of distinct values — about 0.8% error. Returns the estimate. Pass <code>{sketch: true}</code> to get the sketch object instead (<code>_type: "hll"</code>, <code>estimate</code>, <code>registers</code>), for <code>SKETCH_MERGE</code>; a sketch passed back in returns its estimate.</p>
                  <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT([1, 1, 2, 3]) --3
LET a = APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT(xs, {sketch: true})
LET b = APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT(ys, {sketch: true})
RETURN SKETCH_MERGE(a, b).estimate</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div id="fn-APPROX_PERCENTILE" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                  <a href="#fn-APPROX_PERCENTILE" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-blue-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                  <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">APPROX_PERCENTILE(arr, p)</code>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Despite the name, exact: the numeric value at index round(p/100 · (n − 1)) of the sorted array. <code>p</code> is 0-100.</p>
                  <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN APPROX_PERCENTILE([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 50) --3</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div id="fn-APPROX_TOP_K" class="group relative bg-gray-800/40 border border-white/5 rounded-xl p-5 hover:bg-gray-800/60 transition-colors">
                  <a href="#fn-APPROX_TOP_K" class="absolute top-3 right-3 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 text-gray-500 hover:text-blue-400 transition-opacity"><i class="fas fa-link text-xs"></i></a>
                  <code class="block text-sm font-bold text-white bg-blue-500/20 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-blue-500/30 w-fit mb-3">APPROX_TOP_K(arr, k)</code>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">Most frequent values as <code>[{value, count}]</code>, most frequent first. Uses a table of 4k counters (Misra-Gries): counts are exact while the number of distinct values fits, lower bounds after. <code>k</code> is capped at 10,000.</p>
                  <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-2 rounded border border-white/5">RETURN APPROX_TOP_K(["a", "a", "b", "a", "c"], 2)
-- [{value: "a", count: 3}, {value: "b", count: 1}]</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Window Functions -->
      <section id="window-functions" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-900/40 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/10 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-2xl">
          <div class="p-8 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white flex items-center">Window Functions</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="p-8 space-y-4">
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
              <code>FUNC(args) OVER (PARTITION BY … ORDER BY …)</code> computes a value per row from the rows
              of its partition without collapsing them. See also the
              <a href="/docs/sdbql-syntax#window-functions" class="text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300">syntax guide</a>.
            </p>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <tbody>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">ROW_NUMBER()</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">1, 2, 3… in window order.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">RANK() / DENSE_RANK()</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Rank with gaps / without gaps; rows with equal ORDER BY values are peers.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">PERCENT_RANK()</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">(rank − 1) / (rows − 1); 0 for a single row.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">CUME_DIST()</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Rows up to and including the current row's last peer, over rows.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">NTILE(n)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Bucket number 1…n; bucket sizes differ by at most one, larger buckets first.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">LAG(expr<br>offset?<br>default?) / LEAD(...)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Value <code>offset</code> rows before / after (default 1), or <code>default</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">FIRST_VALUE(expr) / LAST_VALUE(expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">First / last value of the whole partition.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">NTH_VALUE(expr<br>n)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">Value of the n-th row (1-based) of the whole partition, or <code>null</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-orange-300">SUM / AVG / COUNT / MIN / MAX (expr)</td><td class="py-2 text-gray-400">With ORDER BY: running value from the partition start to the current row. Without ORDER BY: the whole partition.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <pre><code class="language-sql text-xs bg-gray-950/50 p-3 rounded border border-white/5">FOR s IN scores
  RETURN {
    name: s.name,
    quartile: NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY s.score DESC),
    pct: PERCENT_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY s.score),
    second_best: NTH_VALUE(s.score, 2) OVER (ORDER BY s.score DESC)
  }</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ROLLUP / CUBE -->
      <section id="rollup-cube" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="bg-gray-900/40 backdrop-blur-sm border border-white/10 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-2xl">
          <div class="p-8 border-b border-white/5 bg-white/5">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white flex items-center">
              <span class="bg-orange-500/20 text-orange-300 text-xs font-mono px-2 py-1 rounded mr-3">ROLLUP</span>
              Subtotals
            </h2>
          </div>
          <div class="p-8 space-y-6">
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
              <code class="text-orange-300">ROLLUP</code> and <code class="text-orange-300">CUBE</code> are written once, immediately after the group variables and before <code>INTO</code>, <code>WITH COUNT INTO</code>, <code>AGGREGATE</code> and <code>OPTIONS</code>. They add rows beyond the detail groups. A variable that was rolled up is JSON <code>null</code> on that row. <code>GROUPING(name)</code> is 1 when that variable was rolled up and 0 when the value is a real group key, which is how a stored null is told from a subtotal.
            </p>
            <div class="bg-gray-950/50 rounded-xl border border-white/5 p-6">
              <pre><code class="language-sql text-sm">FOR o IN orders
  COLLECT country = o.country, status = o.status
  ROLLUP
  AGGREGATE total = SUM(o.amount)
  RETURN {
    country,
    status,
    total,
    country_rolled: GROUPING(country),
    status_rolled: GROUPING(status)
  }</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
              Three orders, <code>(US, open, 10)</code>, <code>(US, paid, 5)</code> and <code>(FR, open, 7)</code>, come back as six rows. Detail groups first (both <code>GROUPING</code> values 0), then each country with <code>status</code> rolled up, then the grand total:
            </p>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                  <tr class="text-left text-gray-500">
                    <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">country</th>
                    <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">status</th>
                    <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">total</th>
                    <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">GROUPING(country)</th>
                    <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">GROUPING(status)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="text-gray-300">
                  <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4">FR</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">open</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">7</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">0</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">0</td></tr>
                  <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4">US</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">open</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">10</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">0</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">0</td></tr>
                  <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4">US</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">paid</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">5</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">0</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">0</td></tr>
                  <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4">FR</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-gray-500">null</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">7</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">0</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1</td></tr>
                  <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4">US</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-gray-500">null</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">15</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">0</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1</td></tr>
                  <tr class="border-t border-white/5"><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-gray-500">null</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-gray-500">null</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">22</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
              Within one level, keys sort as they do for an ordinary <code>COLLECT</code>, and null sorts below every value, so <code>FR</code> precedes <code>US</code> and a rolled-up <code>status</code> follows the real statuses only because it is a later level. The grand total is last.
            </p>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white">ROLLUP and CUBE</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
              For group variables <code>(a, b, c)</code>, bit <code>i</code> of a row&rsquo;s mask is set when variable <code>i</code> was rolled up. The detail groups are mask 0.
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-5">
              <li><code>ROLLUP</code> is the <code>n + 1</code> prefixes. With three variables the masks are 0 (detail), then 4 (only <code>c</code>), then 6 (<code>b</code> and <code>c</code>), then 7 (the grand total). Each step rolls one more variable off the end. Two variables, as in the table, are masks 0, 2 and 3.</li>
              <li><code>CUBE</code> is every subset: masks <code>1</code> through <code>2ⁿ − 1</code>, plus the detail rows. The same three orders also produce the rows where <code>country</code> is rolled up and <code>status</code> is not: <code>(null, open, 17)</code> with <code>GROUPING</code> values 1 and 0, and <code>(null, paid, 5)</code>. Eight rows in all, still ordered by mask and then by key.</li>
              <li>The two keywords are exclusive. A second one, or both, is a parse error: <code>ROLLUP and CUBE each appear once, after the group variables, and not together</code>.</li>
              <li>No group variables is already a single group, so <code>ROLLUP</code> or <code>CUBE</code> adds nothing and the row matches an ordinary <code>COLLECT</code> of the same aggregates. <code>GROUPING</code> of a name that is not a group variable is an error on that row too.</li>
              <li>An empty input with one or more group variables yields no rows. There is no grand total of nothing. The same <code>COLLECT</code> with no group variables still returns its one row (<code>COUNT()</code> is 0, <code>SUM</code> of nothing is 0).</li>
              <li>At most 31 group variables. One more is an error, <code>ROLLUP and CUBE accept at most 31 group variables</code>, even when the input is empty. <code>GROUPING</code> only covers those 31.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white">GROUPING()</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
              <code class="text-orange-300">GROUPING(country)</code> takes the group variable&rsquo;s name, not an expression. <code>GROUPING(o.country)</code> fails: <code>GROUPING() takes a group variable name, not an expression</code>. A name the <code>COLLECT</code> did not declare fails with <code>GROUPING(city) is not a group variable of this COLLECT</code>. Written outside a <code>COLLECT</code> it fails with <code>GROUPING() is only valid on the rows of a COLLECT</code>. It is not a general function: the argument is not evaluated, because evaluating it would pass the group&rsquo;s value and lose the name.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
              On an ordinary <code>COLLECT</code>, with no <code>ROLLUP</code> or <code>CUBE</code>, every <code>GROUPING</code> of a declared variable is 0. That includes a count answered from an index, which has no rolled-up rows.
            </p>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white">What a subtotal holds</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-5">
              <li>Each aggregate is merged from the detail groups that share the kept keys. <code>COUNT</code>, <code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>, <code>COUNT_DISTINCT</code>, the variance family, <code>MEDIAN</code>, <code>PERCENTILE</code>, <code>APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT</code> and <code>BIT_AND</code> / <code>BIT_OR</code> / <code>BIT_XOR</code> merge as they do across a parallel scan. Of two equal extremes, or of <code>1</code> and <code>1.0</code>, the value from the earlier row is the one kept.</li>
              <li><code>COLLECT_LIST</code> (and <code>PUSH</code>) appends the detail lists in the order those groups were created. <code>UNIQUE</code> and <code>SORTED_UNIQUE</code> insert values that are not already present, in that same group-by-group order (not the order the rows arrived in: rows A1, B1, A2 give A1, A2, B1); <code>SORTED_UNIQUE</code> sorts at the end, as it does for a detail group. A country subtotal of two amounts 10 and 5, the open order stored first, is <code>[10, 5]</code>.</li>
              <li><code>INTO g</code> on a subtotal is the member rows of its detail groups concatenated, in the order those groups were created. A grand total&rsquo;s <code>INTO</code> therefore holds every input row. That multiplies memory: each detail row is stored again in every super-group that covers it. <code>WITH COUNT INTO</code> adds the detail counts.</li>
              <li>The extra rows are built when the grouping finishes. A scan, an index fold, a column-block fold and a columnar fold all go through that finish, so the subtotals match. Shards of a sharded columnar collection send their detail groups only; the subtotals are built after those groups have been merged, and a group&rsquo;s total is every shard&rsquo;s. A <code>ROLLUP</code> or <code>CUBE</code> with group variables is not answered by counting an index, and the ungrouped form of the columnar array fold steps aside (the one group is the same either way).</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">
              <code>EXPLAIN</code> mentions the clause in <code>warnings</code>: prefixes and a grand total for <code>ROLLUP</code>, every subset for <code>CUBE</code>, what <code>GROUPING</code> returns, and that <code>INTO</code> members of a subtotal are concatenated.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Navigation -->
      <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-8 border-t border-white/10">
        <a href="/docs/sdbql-graphs" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-white transition-colors">
          <span class="flex items-center">
            <svg class="w-4 h-4 mr-2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"></path>
            </svg>
            Graph Queries
          </span>
        </a>
        <a href="/docs/sdbql-functions-string" class="text-indigo-400 hover:text-indigo-300 transition-colors">
          <span class="flex items-center">
            String Functions
            <svg class="w-4 h-4 ml-2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"></path>
            </svg>
          </span>
        </a>
      </div>

    </main>
  </div>
</div>
